> **TL;DR:** The 1% risk rule means risking no more than 1% of your total trading capital on any single trade, regardless of how confident you feel about the setup. It works because it protects you from the compounding math of drawdowns — a loss of 50% requires a 100% gain to recover, and position sizing is what keeps you far away from that hole.

You nailed the entry. Clean breakout, volume confirmation, textbook setup — and you still lost 8% of your account in one trade because you sized it like it was a sure thing. This is the part traders skip when they study charts: the entry was never the problem. The size was.

## The Root Problem

The retail obsession with entries exists because entries feel controllable and satisfying — you drew the trendline, you spotted the pattern, you were "right." Position sizing feels boring by comparison: it's just a number you calculate before the trade even starts. But entries only determine whether you're right or wrong on a given trade; position sizing determines how much that being-wrong actually costs you, and that's the number that decides whether you're still trading six months from now.

The 1% risk rule formalizes this: on any single trade, the maximum you're willing to lose (not the capital deployed, but the actual loss if your stop is hit) should not exceed 1% of your total trading capital. On a ₹5,00,000 account, that's ₹5,000 per trade — not ₹5,000 worth of shares, but ₹5,000 as your worst-case loss if the stop triggers.

## The Breakdown

### The drawdown math that makes 1% non-negotiable

The core reason position sizing outweighs entry quality is asymmetric recovery math. Losses and the gains needed to recover from them are not symmetric — they get exponentially worse as the loss deepens.

| Drawdown | Gain Required to Recover |
|---|---|
| 10% | 11.1% |
| 20% | 25% |
| 30% | 42.9% |
| 50% | 100% |
| 70% | 233% |
| 90% | 900% |

A trader risking 1% per trade needs roughly 50+ consecutive losses to hit a 40% drawdown — a near-impossible streak with any functioning setup. A trader risking 10% per trade hits the same drawdown in just 4-5 bad trades, which is a normal losing streak for even a good strategy with a 50% win rate. The entry quality is identical in both scenarios; the position size is what determines whether the losing streak is a footnote or an account-ending event.

